  "final_span": "able to identify genetic loci that contribute to the environmentally sensitive germination of high-temperature-sensitive germination type.\nDr. Bjorndal currently manages the projects for 4 students involved in the NSF-funded Plant Ontology Project, with most students working alongside at the USDA Plant Gene Expression Center.\nDr. Cristie and ex-student Jesse Nusinow are engaged in a project to identify novel molecular biology tribes (MBTs) within plant flowering genes.\nDr. Benzinger, Dr. Johnson, along with postdoctoral researchers Yue Dai and Joseph Cheriyath, are working to identify biomarkers for the obscure wheat yellow stripe virus (WYSV) to help mitigate its impact on wheat production.\nDr. Bugel and former postdoc Jesse Nusinow are determining or augmenting a previously identified MYB transcription factor family for desirable traits in wheat.\nDr. Benzinger supervises PhD student Sun Chang on a project aimed at developing effective molecular techniques for identifying and characterizing wheat and related species.\nDr. Batterson, Dr. Benzinger along with postdoc Mia Hall are expanding a collection of aluminum-resistant genomes from prior studies to offer broader aluminum stress tolerance in wheat.\nDr. Bugel and postdoc Jiafei Wang are working to identify wheat alleles encoding a new soybean nodule promoter for use in analyzing wheat gene expression.\nUnder Dr. Johnson\u2019s supervision, the PhD student Zachary Shively, pre-doctoral intern Yan Lin, along with postdoctoral researchers Jiafei Wang and Valerie Ayres, are working on projects aimed at facilitating genetic engineering and genomics endeavors in wheat through a range of engineering and genomics tools available at the International Wheat Genome Sequencing Consortium/SciLifeLab facility in Uppsala, Sweden.\nDr. Bjorndal is overseeing PhD student Aaron Dodds in a project to identify genetic loci involved in internal pathway responsiveness for nitrogen in wheat and to utilize functions of these loci in genetic engineering approaches to breeding for genotypes and phenot",
